## Responsive Design

**Components scale against their container, not the screen.** This is a chat
library: on a laptop the chat panel is 400px wide while the screen is 1440px. A
screen query (`md:`) would hand every card inside that panel its desktop padding
and type, in a box no wider than a phone. So content is sized with Tailwind v4
**container queries** — `@md:` instead of `md:` — which ask "how wide is the box
I am in?" instead.

**Mobile-first, always.** Default styles are the compact size. Scale up with `@md:`
and `@3xl:`.

### Breakpoints
| Variant | Container width | Use for |
|---------|-----------------|---------|
| default | < 448px | Phone, chat panel, narrow sheet — design here first |
| `@md:` | 448px+ (28rem) | Roomy box — tablet, wide modal, consumer page column |
| `@3xl:` | 768px+ (48rem) | Full-width page area — rare inside a component |

The two thresholds are Tailwind's stock container sizes. Do not redefine
`--container-*` to move them: the same variables drive `max-w-md` etc.

### Three axes, not one

| Axis | Query | Governs |
|------|-------|---------|
| **Container** | `@md:` `@3xl:` | Type scale, padding, gaps, icon size, column counts — everything inside a component |
| **Viewport** | `md:` `lg:` | Only frames anchored to the screen: a `fixed` sheet becoming a centred modal, `100dvh` heights, the drag handle that only a bottom sheet has |
| **Input device** | `pointer-coarse:` `pointer-fine:` | Hit area — and nothing else |

If the class would still make sense with the component dropped into a 360px
sidebar on a 4K monitor, it is a container class. `md:` is the exception and
needs a reason a reviewer can see.

### Who declares the container

A container query asks the **nearest ancestor** marked `@container`. A component
never queries itself, so its own root padding responds to the box *around* it.

- **Layouts declare it.** `ChatWidget`'s root, `ModalSheet`'s panel and
  `ApplePaySheet`'s sheet carry `@container`, so everything inside them scales
  to the panel.
- **Consumers declare it** on whatever area holds loose components:
  `<main className="@container">`. With no container anywhere above, a component
  stays at its compact size — a safe default, never a broken one.
- **Storybook declares it** in `.storybook/preview.tsx`, so the viewport toolbar
  and Chromatic's 375px / 1280px snapshots reach every story. A story's own
  width wrapper (`max-w-sm`) must **not** add `@container` — it would pin the
  component to its compact size at every viewport. Only a wrapper that stands in
  for a chat panel (`ChatShell` in `RichContent.stories.tsx`) should.
- **Components do not declare it** on their roots. A nested container would
  silently change what every descendant measures against.
  *Exception:* a self-sizing tile that lays out its own insides by its own width
  (`StatCard`, with `@[8rem]:`-style queries). It may be its own container,
  because nothing passed into it needs to scale to the page.

Two traps:

1. **A container must have a definite width.** `container-type: inline-size`
   ignores its content when sizing, so a shrink-wrapped container (inline, a
   flex item with no width, `w-fit`) collapses to 0. Put it only on block boxes
   that fill their parent or have an explicit width.
2. **`@md:` without an ancestor never matches.** That is why the compact size
   must be a complete design on its own.

### Spacing (Progressive Scaling — no big jumps)
| Context | Compact → @md → @3xl |
|---------|---------------------|
| Card padding | `p-4 @md:p-6 @3xl:p-8` |
| Section gaps | `gap-4 @md:gap-6 @3xl:gap-8` |
| Inner gaps | `gap-2 @md:gap-3` |
| Button groups | `gap-2 @md:gap-3` |

**Rule**: `px-4 @md:px-6` ✅ · `px-4 @3xl:px-12` ❌

### What does not scale

Leave these fixed — a scaled version looks wrong, not bigger:

- Hairlines and borders, status dots, progress bars, skeleton line heights
- Third-party marks and native replicas (Apple Pay sheet type, payment logos)
- Sizes a prop already chooses (`Avatar size="sm"`)
- `components/ui/button.tsx` CVA sizes — call sites state their own height
- Hit areas — those belong to `pointer-coarse:`, below

### Icons

Icons scale with the text beside them: `size-4 @md:size-5` next to body text,
`size-3.5 @md:size-4` next to captions. Pass the size as a class, not as
lucide's `size={18}` prop, so the `@md:` partner is possible.

### Minimum hit area (mandatory on every interactive element)

| Pointer | Minimum | Comfortable | Standard |
|---------|---------|-------------|----------|
| Coarse (finger, stylus) | **44 × 44 px** | 48 × 48 | Apple HIG · WCAG 2.5.5 (AAA) |
| Fine (mouse, trackpad) | **24 × 24 px** | 32 × 32 | WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8 (AA) |
| Gap between adjacent targets | **8 px** | 12 px | Material |

Both minimums are exposed as `--tap-target-min`, which resolves to 24px under a
fine pointer and 44px under a coarse one. You rarely read it directly — the
`tap-target` utility does.

### Which mechanism to use

Decide by asking: **may this control get visually bigger under a finger?**

**Yes — it can grow.** Size it with a `pointer-coarse:` variant. Standalone
buttons, steppers, close buttons, anything whose box is the affordance.

```tsx
// Full-width CTA — 48 on phone, 40 for a cursor, never under 44 for a finger
<Button className="w-full h-12 @md:h-10 pointer-coarse:min-h-11">Confirm</Button>

// Icon button
<Button className="size-12 @md:size-10 pointer-coarse:size-12" aria-label="Close" />
```

Prefer `min-h-11` over `h-11` in the coarse branch: `min-height` beats `height`
regardless of which utility Tailwind emits first, so the rule can't lose a
specificity race with `@md:`.

**No — its size is the design.** Use `tap-target`, which expands the hit area
with a centred pseudo-element while the visual box stays put. Switch tracks, tag
removes, inline icon affordances, message feedback buttons.

```tsx
// A 14px remove affordance that still accepts a 44px finger
<Button className="size-3.5 @md:size-3 rounded-full p-0 tap-target" aria-label="Remove" />
```

Two limits on `tap-target`, both of which will bite silently:

1. It sets `position: relative` — **never put it on an absolutely positioned
   element**, it will override the placement. Those can always grow, so use the
   `pointer-coarse:` route instead.
2. The hit area extends past the visual box, so **adjacent controls closer than
   `--tap-target-min` apart will overlap**, and the later sibling wins every tap.
   Carousel dots 6px apart are the classic case: 44px pseudo areas make the last
   dot swallow the whole strip. Tightly packed controls need real tiling —
   a `size-11` button with the visual as an inner element — not a pseudo area.

Rule of thumb: `tap-target` for an *isolated* small control, real sizing for a
*row* of them.

### Verifying

`node scripts/tap-audit.js` scans every component for controls that fall below
the minimum on either pointer. It must report zero before a component ships.
Chrome DevTools device emulation reports `pointer: coarse`, so both branches are
testable without a physical device.

### Typography (Progressive)
| Element | Classes |
|---------|---------|
| Display / Hero | `text-3xl @md:text-4xl font-semibold` |
| H1 | `text-2xl @md:text-3xl font-semibold` |
| H2 | `text-xl @md:text-2xl font-semibold` |
| H3 | `text-base @md:text-lg font-semibold` |
| Body | `text-sm @md:text-base` |
| Caption | `text-xs @md:text-sm` |
| Amount hero | `text-3xl @md:text-4xl font-semibold tabular-nums` |

> **Fluid alternative**: `text-[clamp(1.5rem,6cqi,2.25rem)]` scales smoothly with the container (`cqi` = 1% of the container's width) — splash/onboarding text only; components use the table above for predictability.

**Note on native-replica components** (ApplePaySheet): type that copies a platform's own sheet may use a fixed size class (e.g. `text-[25px]`) — the platform does not scale it either. All other dimensions must still use Tailwind classes, not inline styles.

Content widths: `max-w-prose` (reading), `max-w-4xl` (forms), `max-w-7xl` (dashboard)

### Chat Window Sizing
The chat window frame is the one place a viewport query is right — it decides
whether the widget *is* the screen:

```tsx
// Full screen on mobile, floating panel on larger screens — viewport, because the frame is anchored to the screen
<div className="
  @container                             // and everything inside scales to the panel
  fixed inset-0
  md:relative md:inset-auto
  md:w-[400px] lg:w-[440px]
  h-[100dvh] md:h-[600px] lg:h-[680px]
">
```

Message bubbles: `max-w-[75%] @md:max-w-sm` — constrain width to preserve readability.

### Layout Patterns
```tsx
// Side-by-side in a roomy box, stacked in a narrow one
<div className="flex flex-col @md:flex-row gap-4 @md:gap-6">

// Grid: 1 col compact, 2 col roomy, 3 col full width
<div className="grid grid-cols-1 @md:grid-cols-2 @3xl:grid-cols-3 gap-4 @md:gap-6">

// Hide in a narrow box
<div className="hidden @md:block">
```

### Verifying width

- Storybook's viewport toolbar has Phone / Tablet / Desktop. Check all three.
- Chromatic snapshots every story at 375px and 1280px, so a layout that breaks
  on a phone shows up as a visual diff.

